.TH smi-incoming-deploy "1" "October 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-incoming-deploy \- Install incoming deployment packages, as a draft or published
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-incoming-deploy
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-incoming-deploy
installs the RPM packages an external system drops into the incoming directories.
It is started by
.B setmy-info-deploy.path
as soon as a package appears, so a deployment needs nothing but the upload.
.SH STAGES
.PP
A package is deployed into one of two stages, and the external system chooses by the directory it
uploads into. That is the whole interface it needs: it uploads a file and nothing else, it sets no
variable and runs no command, because the directory is the signal.
.PP
.RS
.nf
/var/opt/setmy.info/incoming/*.rpm         published
/var/opt/setmy.info/incoming/draft/*.rpm   draft
.fi
.RE
.PP
Both are deployed on the same machine, the live one; the stage is only how far the deployment has
got. A draft is checked by whoever approves it, and the external system publishes it by moving
the same file to
.IR /var/opt/setmy.info/incoming ,
or rejects it by deleting it. Nothing on this machine approves or rejects.
.PP
The stage reaches the package in the
.SM SMI_STAGE
environment variable,
.B published
or
.BR draft .
.B rpm
runs the scriptlets of a package as child shells and passes its own environment on, so
.B %pre
and
.B %post
of the package read
.SM SMI_STAGE
and put the content where that stage belongs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
case "${SMI_STAGE:-published}" in
    draft)     TARGET=/usr/share/nginx/vhosts/HOST/draft/apps/APP ;;
    published) TARGET=/usr/share/nginx/vhosts/HOST/apps/APP ;;
esac
.fi
.RE
.PP
An unset
.SM SMI_STAGE
means published, which the
.B :-published
of that
.B case
gives: a package installed by hand with
.B rpm -U
or
.BR "dnf install" ,
without the variable, publishes. Only a deployment that says
.B draft
is a draft.
.PP
One package therefore serves both stages.
The file that is uploaded to the draft directory, checked there and then moved to the published
directory is one and the same file, so what is published is what was approved, and nothing is
built twice.
.PP
Drafts are deployed before the published packages of the same run, so a package that is in both
directories at once ends with the published one in place.
.SH INSTALLATION
.PP
Each package is installed with
.PP
.RS
.nf
SMI_STAGE=STAGE rpm -U --replacepkgs --oldpackage PACKAGE
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B --replacepkgs
because publishing a checked draft installs the version that is already installed, which rpm
would otherwise refuse, and
.B --oldpackage
so a deliberate step back to an earlier version works as well.
.PP
rpm keeps one instance of a package name, so the draft and the published deployment are the same
installation seen twice: the files of the package are installed once and the scriptlets place or
link the content of the stage they were run for. A package that keeps its payload in a versioned
directory and links the stage directory to it can therefore hold both stages at once, on
different versions.
.PP
The package file belongs to this command:
.IP \(bu 2
installed: the file is removed
.IP \(bu 2
not installed: the file is moved to
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/failed
or
.IR /var/opt/setmy.info/failed/draft ,
the failed directory of its stage, with a UTC timestamp in front of its name
.PP
Every step and every reason is written to standard error, which
.BR journalctl (1)
keeps for the service.
.PP
The incoming directories are empty when the command ends.
That is not tidiness:
.B setmy-info-deploy.path
triggers on the incoming globs, so a package left there would start the deployment again and
again, burning processor time until systemd's start limit stopped it.
.SH LIMITS
.PP
The staging only exists for packages that implement it.
A package that does not read
.SM SMI_STAGE
ignores it: dropped into
.IR /var/opt/setmy.info/incoming/draft ,
it is given
.BR SMI_STAGE=draft ,
pays no attention, and installs exactly as it would have from the published directory, so it goes
live at once.
Nothing here refuses such a package, because nothing here knows what a package puts where.
An upload into the draft directory that has to be refused rather than silently published needs a
check of its own, a marker in the package or a naming convention this command tests, and that is
not implemented.
.PP
The two
.B rpm
options apply to every package, not only to the one being published:
.B --replacepkgs
lets the version that is already installed be installed again, and
.B --oldpackage
permits a downgrade, so an older package uploaded into either directory is installed over a newer
one without a word.
The version that is deployed is the version that was uploaded, and nothing here compares it with
what is installed.
.PP
.B rpm -U
resolves no dependencies.
A package that needs something which is not installed yet fails and is moved to the failed
directory, where
.BR dnf (8)
would have pulled the dependency in.
A package that brings its own requirements, or whose requirements are already on the machine, is
what this command is for; anything else is installed by hand.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every package was installed and removed, or there was nothing to do.
.TP
.B 1
A package could not be installed and was moved to the failed directory, or a package could not be
removed from or moved out of an incoming directory. The packages after it were still processed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/incoming
Published packages, uploaded there by the external system.
Created by the installation of this toolset, owned by
.B deploy
so that the external system can upload into it.
.TP
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/incoming/draft
Draft packages, the same owner.
.TP
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/failed
.TQ
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/failed/draft
Packages that could not be installed, one directory per stage, each package with a UTC timestamp
in front of its name. Nothing prunes them.
.TP
.I /opt/setmy.info/etc/systemd/system/setmy-info-deploy.path
.TQ
.I /opt/setmy.info/etc/systemd/system/setmy-info-deploy.service
The watcher and the service it starts, linked into
.I /etc/systemd/system
by the rpm and deb install scripts.
.SH NOTES
.PP
A machine is ready to receive deployments as soon as this toolset is installed: the installation
creates the two incoming directories and the two failed directories, creates the
.B deploy
user when it does not exist, gives it the incoming directories, and enables the watcher. What is
left is the public key of the external system in
.IR /home/deploy/.ssh/authorized_keys ,
which belongs to whoever owns that system.
.PP
Upload a package under a different name or into another directory and move it in when it is
complete: the watcher reacts to the file as soon as it exists, and rpm refuses a package that is
still being written.
.PP
The machines of this toolset are Rocky Linux and Fedora, so the packages are RPMs. A Debian based
machine needs the two
.B .deb
globs of
.B setmy-info-deploy.path
and the two
.B dpkg
lines of this command uncommented and the
.B .rpm
ones commented, and nothing else:
.BR dpkg (1)
passes its environment to the maintainer scripts the same way
.B rpm
does, so
.SM SMI_STAGE
reaches
.B preinst
and
.B postinst
unchanged. That is prepared, not tested.
.PP
.B sudo
removes the environment of the caller, so
.SM SMI_STAGE
would not reach the scriptlets through
.BR "sudo rpm" .
This command runs as root from the service and sets the variable itself, which is why it works
here; by hand it is
.B SMI_STAGE=draft rpm -U ...
as root, or
.B sudo -E
with the variable kept in
.IR /etc/sudoers .
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
What the external system does for a draft, and later for publishing it:
.RS
.nf
smi-upload app-1.4.2.rpm incoming/draft/app-1.4.2.rpm deploy@server
smi-upload app-1.4.2.rpm incoming/app-1.4.2.rpm deploy@server
.fi
.RE
.PP
What was deployed, and what failed:
.RS
.nf
journalctl -u setmy-info-deploy.service
ls -l /var/opt/setmy.info/failed /var/opt/setmy.info/failed/draft
.fi
.RE
